<%- include('../partials/header') %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>Import members from a spreadsheet</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    The parish already holds its families somewhere &mdash; a register, a
    ledger, somebody&rsquo;s spreadsheet. Nobody should type it in twice.
    Download the sheet below, fill it in, and upload it here.
  </p>
</div>

<%
  /*
   * The outcome of an upload, at the top of the page rather than beside the
   * button, because it is the only thing the person is looking for when the
   * page comes back and it is longer than the button is.
   *
   * Either `problems` or `result` is filled in, never both: a sheet with a
   * single problem in it imports nothing at all.
   */
%>
<% if (problems.length) { %>
  <div class="alert error">
    <strong>
      Nothing was imported<%= fileName ? ` from ${fileName}` : '' %>.
      <% if (problemCount > 1) { %>
        There are <%= problemCount %> things to fix first.
      <% } %>
    </strong>
    <ul>
      <% problems.forEach(function (problem) { %>
        <li>
          <% if (problem.line) { %><strong>Row <%= problem.line %>:</strong> <% } %>
          <%= problem.message %>
        </li>
      <% }); %>
      <% if (problemsHidden) { %>
        <li>&hellip;and <%= problemsHidden %> more. Fix these first &mdash; the same
          mistake usually explains the rest.</li>
      <% } %>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">
      Correct those rows in your spreadsheet, save it again as
      <code>.csv</code>, and upload it once more. The directory has not been
      changed, so there is nothing to undo.
    </p>
  </div>
<% } %>

<% if (result) { %>
  <div class="alert notice">
    <strong>
      Imported <%= result.families %> famil<%= result.families === 1 ? 'y' : 'ies' %><%
        %><%= result.people ? ` and ${result.people} people` : '' %><%
        %><%= fileName ? ` from ${fileName}` : '' %>.
    </strong>
    <p style="margin:8px 0 0">
      They arrived as <strong>drafts</strong>, so nothing has entered the
      printed book yet &mdash; look them over in
      <a href="<%= base %>/families">Families</a> and publish the
      ones that are right. <strong>No logins were created:</strong> accounts
      stay a separate, deliberate step on
      <a href="<%= base %>/admin/users">User accounts</a>.
    </p>

    <% if (result.adjusted.length) { %>
      <p style="margin:10px 0 4px">Decisions made for you, so you can check them:</p>
      <ul>
        <% result.adjusted.forEach(function (item) { %>
          <li><%= item.message %></li>
        <% }); %>
      </ul>
    <% } %>

    <% if (result.unexpected.length) { %>
      <p style="margin:10px 0 4px">
        These were checked as fine a moment earlier and then were not &mdash;
        somebody else was very likely working on the directory at the same
        time. Nothing was overwritten:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <% result.unexpected.forEach(function (item) { %>
          <li><%= item.message %></li>
        <% }); %>
      </ul>
    <% } %>
  </div>
<% } %>

<% if (unknownColumns.length) { %>
  <div class="alert error" style="background:var(--paper-warm);border-color:var(--rule);color:var(--ink)">
    <strong>Columns this directory has no home for</strong>
    <p style="margin:6px 0 0">
      <% unknownColumns.forEach(function (name, i) { %><code><%= name %></code><%= i < unknownColumns.length - 1 ? ', ' : '' %><% }); %>
      <%= unknownColumns.length === 1 ? 'was' : 'were' %> not recognised as
      <%= unknownColumns.length === 1 ? 'a heading' : 'headings' %> this
      directory stores, so whatever is in
      <%= unknownColumns.length === 1 ? 'it' : 'them' %>
      <%= result ? 'has not been kept' : 'would not be kept' %>. This is
      reported rather than passed over in silence; it is not itself a reason to
      stop. If one of them is a column this directory does hold under another
      name, the table below says what to call it.
    </p>
  </div>
<% } %>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Get the template</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    A comma-separated file, which Excel, LibreOffice and Google Sheets all open
    by double-clicking. Fill it in there and save it back as
    <code>.csv</code> &mdash; save it as <code>.xlsx</code> and it can no
    longer be read.
  </p>

  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn" href="<%= base %>/admin/import-template.csv">Template with example rows</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="<%= base %>/admin/import-template.csv?examples=0">
      Headings only
    </a>
  </div>

  <p class="help">
    The example rows are there to be read and then deleted. The headings-only
    file is the same sheet with nothing under them.
  </p>
</div>

<form class="card" method="post" action="<%= base %>/admin/import"
      enctype="multipart/form-data">
  <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value="<%= csrfToken %>">

  <h2>Upload the filled-in sheet</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    The whole file is read and checked before anything is written. If there is
    a problem anywhere in it &mdash; a date nobody could read, an email address
    that is not one, a mobile number too short to ring, a family with no name,
    a Family ID this parish already has &mdash; it is listed at the top of this
    page in plain words, with the row number, and
    <strong>nothing at all is imported</strong>. Fix the sheet and upload it
    again. There is no half-finished state to clean up.
  </p>

  <div class="field">
    <label for="sheet">Your spreadsheet, saved as CSV</label>
    <input type="file" id="sheet" name="sheet" accept=".csv,text/csv" required>
    <p class="help">
      A <code>.csv</code> file, up to <%= maxSheetMb %>&nbsp;MB. An Excel
      <code>.xlsx</code> workbook cannot be read &mdash; open it, choose
      File&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;Save&nbsp;As and pick
      &ldquo;CSV (comma delimited)&rdquo;.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="actions">
    <button type="submit" class="btn">Check and import</button>
  </div>
</form>

<div class="card">
  <h2>The one rule that matters</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>One row per person, not one row per family.</strong> A family of
    five is five rows carrying the same <strong>Family ID</strong>, and that
    column is what ties them together. It is the only column the import
    refuses a sheet without.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    The family&rsquo;s own details &mdash; address, prayer group, date of
    marriage &mdash; are read from the <em>first</em> row carrying that Family
    ID. Leave them blank on the rest of the household, or repeat them; the
    sheet is not wrong either way.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    Two families must never share a Family ID, and a Family ID this parish
    already holds stops the import and is reported with its row number. Take
    that family&rsquo;s rows out of the sheet, or give the new family a Family
    ID of its own &mdash; a record the office has corrected by hand is never
    overwritten by an import somebody runs twice.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>The columns</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    Column order does not matter, and neither does letter case, spacing or
    punctuation in the headings &mdash; &ldquo;Family ID&rdquo;,
    &ldquo;family_id&rdquo; and &ldquo;FAMILY&nbsp;&nbsp;ID.&rdquo; are one
    column. If the parish sheet already calls a column something else, check
    the alternatives here before renaming anything: it is probably recognised
    as it stands. A column that is recognised by no heading at all is reported
    when the sheet is read, never dropped in silence.
  </p>

  <table class="table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Heading in the template</th>
        <th>Also accepted as</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% fields.forEach((field) => { %>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <strong><%= columnLabels[field] %></strong>
            <% if (required.includes(field)) { %>
              <span class="tag">required</span>
            <% } %>
          </td>
          <td class="help">
            <%= aliases[field].filter((a) => a !== columnLabels[field].toLowerCase()).join(', ') %>
          </td>
        </tr>
      <% }) %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>Filling in the awkward columns</h2>

  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>Relation.</strong> Use this parish&rsquo;s own codes, the same ones
    the member editor offers:
    <% if (relations.length) { %>
      <% relations.forEach((relation, i) => { %><code><%= relation %></code><%= i < relations.length - 1 ? ', ' : '' %><% }) %>.
    <% } else { %>
      none are set &mdash; add them on the
      <a href="<%= base %>/admin/settings">Settings</a> page first.
    <% } %>
    Change them on Settings and the template you download changes with them.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Dates.</strong> Write them however the register has them.
    <code>02-Aug-1975</code>, <code>2/8/1975</code>, <code>1975-08-02</code>
    and <code>Aug 1975</code> are all read, and where two numbers are
    ambiguous the <em>day comes first</em> &mdash; <code>03/04/1978</code> is
    the third of April. A date that cannot be read at all is reported as a
    rejected row rather than quietly blanked.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Years are dropped.</strong> This directory prints the day and month
    of both dates and stores no year for either &mdash; it says when to wish
    somebody, not how old they are. Write the year if the register has it: it
    is read and then dropped, not refused, so no sheet has to be stripped
    before it is handed over.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Date of marriage</strong> belongs to the member, not the family, so
    it goes on the row of each person who was married &mdash; both partners, and
    both again for a married son or daughter living in the same household.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Email addresses.</strong> There are two columns and they are not the
    same thing. <code>Email</code> is the family&rsquo;s own, and it doubles as
    the household&rsquo;s sign-in name, so a typo there is a family that cannot
    verify its own entry. <code>Emails</code> is the member&rsquo;s, and one
    member may list up to three, separated by commas.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    Both are checked properly rather than loosely: <code>steve@gmail</code> is
    refused, because a bare hostname is a real address on an office network and
    no address at all on the internet. Anything that is not an address &mdash;
    a name, a phone number, a note &mdash; stops the whole file with the row
    number against it.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Mobile numbers.</strong> Ten digits beginning 6, 7, 8 or 9. A
    leading <code>0</code> is taken off rather than refused, spaces and dashes
    are ignored, and a member may list up to three numbers separated by commas.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Head of family.</strong> If it is blank, the first member listed
    for that family is taken as the head, and the import says so. A family with
    no head and no members is refused &mdash; a printed entry has to have a
    title.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Address.</strong> A line break inside the cell is fine &mdash; the
    two-line address you get from pressing Alt+Enter in Excel survives intact.
  </p>

  <p class="help">
    <strong>Photographs</strong> are not part of this sheet. Import the
    families first, then send the pictures as their own folder on
    <a href="<%= base %>/admin/photos">Import photographs</a> &mdash; each file
    named after the Family ID it belongs to. A single late photograph is still
    added from the family&rsquo;s own page.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <h2>What the import will and will not do</h2>
  <p class="help" style="margin-top:0">
    <strong>All of the sheet, or none of it.</strong> The file is read through
    to the end before a single family is written, and one problem anywhere
    stops the lot. A run that says it imported is a run that imported
    everything in the file &mdash; there is never a half-loaded directory to
    work out the shape of afterwards.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <strong>Nothing already here is touched.</strong> A Family ID this parish
    already holds is reported as a problem to fix in the sheet, not merged and
    not overwritten. A correction the office has made by hand cannot be undone
    by an import somebody runs twice.
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    Everything that arrives arrives as a <strong>draft</strong> &mdash; nothing
    enters the printed book until somebody has looked at it &mdash; and
    <strong>no logins are created</strong>. Accounts stay a separate,
    deliberate step on
    <a href="<%= base %>/admin/users">User accounts</a>.
  </p>

<%
  /*
   * The command line, for the person who has a shell.
   *
   * The form above is what a parish administrator uses and it is enough for
   * any sheet a parish actually has. This is still worth printing for a super
   * administrator, because it can do the one thing the form deliberately will
   * not: import what it can and write the rows it could not to a rejects file,
   * which is the tool for a sheet too broken to fix a row at a time.
   *
   * It is inside the isSuperAdmin guard because a parish administrator has no
   * shell — printing a command to them describes a job they cannot do and
   * reads as an instruction they have failed to follow.
   */
%>
<% if (isSuperAdmin) { %>
  <p class="help">
    The same import from a shell, which reports rather than refuses and can
    write out the rows it could not read:
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    <code>npm run import-families -- --church <%= church.slug %> --file parish.csv --dry-run</code>
  </p>
  <p class="help">
    Running it again without <code>--dry-run</code> does the load; adding
    <code>--rejects bad.csv</code> writes out exactly the rows that failed,
    with the reason in the last column, ready to be fixed and imported again.
  </p>
<% } %>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/footer') %>
